12.3  nice and priorities

TL;DR
  - Niceness goes from -20 (greedy) to 19 (polite). The default is 0.
  - A higher niceness gives a process a smaller share of the CPUs when they are
    busy. When they're idle, it changes nothing.
  - Anyone can make their own process nicer; making it greedier needs root.

      nice -n 10 make -j8          # start a build at low priority
      renice -n 5 -p 1234          # change a running process
      ionice -c3 rsync -a src/ dst/  # use the disk only when idle

Pitfalls
  - Niceness only shares the CPU: a nice process can still fill the RAM or keep
    the disk busy.
  - ionice only has an effect with an I/O scheduler that honors priorities, such
    as BFQ: check /sys/block/DEV/queue/scheduler.
  - In top, NI is the niceness and PR the kernel's priority: 20 + niceness for
    normal processes.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 12.3 --why

Related
  Commands  nice, renice, ionice, chrt
  Hub       idk cpu CPU
  See also  idk 14.1 Load average
